steps.body:
Each stage fails in its own way, and its message says which stage it was, which tells you where to look. Press Run on each of these to see the sandbox's g++ messages.

The **preprocessor** fails when it can't find a header, so check the name. g++ says `main.cpp:1:10: fatal error: iostrem: No such file or directory`, meaning file, line, and column:

```cpp error
#include <iostrem>

int main() {
    return 0;
}
```

The **compiler** fails when code breaks the language's rules, here with `main.cpp:4:33: error: expected ';' before 'return'`. Fix the first error first, since one mistake can cause several messages:

```cpp error
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::cout << "Hello" << '\n'
    return 0;
}
```

The **linker** fails when a name is used but never defined. Its messages have no line numbers, because it works on object files, not your text. Misspell `main`, and the startup code has nothing to call:

```cpp harness
{{code}}

int main();
```

```cpp error
#include <iostream>

int mian() {
    return 0;
}
```

g++ names the caller, `_start` in the startup code's object file `crt1.o`, and the missing symbol (a Mac says `Undefined symbols for architecture arm64` and names `_main`):

```text
/usr/bin/ld: /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/crt1.o: in function `_start':
(.text+0x20): undefined reference to `main'
collect2: error: ld returned 1 exit status
```

A **run-time** error appears only when the program runs: the build succeeded, then the program stopped. `std::stoi` turns text into a whole number, and `"abc"` has no digits, so g++'s library reports `terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::invalid_argument'` and aborts the program, which ends with exit code 134:

```cpp error
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::cout << std::stoi("abc") << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

A **warning** means the code builds but looks wrong. Read every one. *Building Projects: Flags, Make, and CMake* turns on many more with `-Wall -Wextra`, and *Debugging: Crashes, Sanitizers, and a Debugger* covers crashes.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::cout << 'OK' << '\n';         // warning: multi-character character constant
    std::cout << "OK" << '\n';         // fixed: text goes in double quotes
    std::cout << "C:\data" << '\n';    // warning: unknown escape sequence: '\d'
    std::cout << "C:\\data" << '\n';   // fixed: \\ is one backslash
    std::cout << "C:\new" << '\n';     // no warning: \n is a real escape
    std::cout << R"(C:\new)" << '\n';  // fixed: a raw string keeps backslashes
    return 0;
}

