steps.body:
When you run `./hello`, the kernel starts the **loader** (`dyld` on macOS, `ld-linux.so` on Linux). It maps the executable and the shared libraries it lists into the new process, connects the names still marked `U`, and jumps to the startup code, which calls `main`.

`main`'s return value becomes the **exit status**, 0 for success. Scripts and build tools check it to decide whether to carry on. Run this, and the output panel shows its exit code, 3:

```cpp error
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::cout << "config file missing" << '\n';
    return 3;
}
```

A process starts with two output streams. **stdout**, which `std::cout` writes to, is for results. **stderr**, which `std::cerr` writes to, is for errors and warnings, so they still reach you when the results go to a file. Run this one to see both, stderr in red:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::cout << "price 101.25" << '\n';
    std::cerr << "warning: feed is 2 seconds behind" << '\n';
    std::cout << "price 101.30" << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

In a terminal, `>` sends only stdout to a file. On this Mac:

```text
$ ./streams > prices.txt
warning: feed is 2 seconds behind
$ cat prices.txt
price 101.25
price 101.30
```

C++ itself is an ISO standard, revised every three years since 2011: C++98, C++03, C++11, C++14, C++17, C++20, C++23. Compilers implement it: **g++** (GCC, usual on Linux), **clang++** (the Mac's), and **MSVC** (`cl`, in Visual Studio on Windows). `__cplusplus` holds the standard a file was built as, such as `201703` for C++17. This course uses C++17 on g++ 10.2, the sandbox's compiler, which supports all of C++17, still the version much production code targets. Plain `clang++` on this Mac still builds C++14 (`__cplusplus` is `201402`), so pass `-std=c++17` (`/std:c++17` with MSVC). *What C++20 and C++23 Add*, in the advanced track, covers the newer ones.

